<h1 id="p:basic-definitions" class="tex-part"><span data-tag="0000">1</span> TQFT basics</h1>
<p>
 In this part, we introduce the basic definitions of TQFT, classify 1- and 2-dimensional TQFTs, and discuss our first example of a TQFT in arbitrary dimension, Dijkgraaf–Witten theory. 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/0001" data-tag="0001">1</a>, we briefly review the main ideas of quantum field theory in physics, most importantly, the path integral formulation of QFT due to Feynman. This chapter is extremely brief and is used as motivation only; no precise definitions or statements are given there. However, we hope that it helps the reader understand the physical roots of TQFT. 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">1</a>, we introduce the key mathematical tools which will be used extensively throughout this book: the notions of a monoidal category and a monoidal functor, and related topics. 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/0012" data-tag="0012">3</a>, we define the category of cobordisms and use it to give a definition of TQFT as a functor $\mathbf{Cob}_ n\to \mathbf{Vec}$. This definition is essentially due to Atiyah. We also study some basic properties of TQFTs, in particular showing that a TQFT $Z$ defines, for any closed $(n-1)$-manifold $N$, an action of the mapping class group $\Gamma (N)=\pi _0(\operatorname{Diff}_+(N))$ on the vector space $Z(N)$. As a baby example, we give a classification of 1-dimensional TQFTs. This is easy, but serves as a foundation for all classification results which appear later in the book, such as the cobordism hypothesis. 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/0021" data-tag="0021">4</a>, we discuss the classification of 2-dimensional TQFTs, showing that such theories are classified by commutative Frobenius algebras. Our approach is based on the use of Morse functions and families of Morse functions (Cerf theory). 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/002T" data-tag="002T">5</a>, we study Frobenius algebras, in particular classifying all semisimple commutative Frobenius algebras and describing the corresponding 2d TQFTs. 
</p>
<p>
In Chapter&#160;<a href="/tag/003K" data-tag="003K">6</a>, we describe the fundamental example of a TQFT: gauge theory with a finite gauge group, also known as Dijkgraaf–Witten theory. This theory can be described in terms of $G$-covers (principal $G$-bundles); we can also relate it to the path integral description of TQFT.  
</p>
